The reported component (sshswitch Layer-3 SVIs for VLAN 5 and VLAN 6) is operating normally. On sshswitch, the SVI interfaces are present and UP: br_v5 has 10.5.0.254/24 and br_v6 has 10.6.0.254/24. Their corresponding VLAN subinterfaces (eth0.5, eth0.6 and bond0.5, bond0.6) are also UP and enslaved to the correct bridges. This indicates the L3 gateway interfaces are active on the switch.

Mechanism check: If SVIs were down, hosts in 10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24 would fail to reach their gateways and inter-VLAN pings would fail. Instead, hosts on VLAN 5 and VLAN 6 can reach their default gateways with 0% loss: pc1 (10.5.0.1) and pc2 (10.5.0.2) successfully ping 10.5.0.254, while pc3 (10.6.0.3) and pc4 (10.6.0.4) successfully ping 10.6.0.254. Cross-VLAN reachability also succeeds (e.g., pc1/pc2 to 172.16.10.8 and 172.16.30.12).

Localization: sshswitch itself shows br_v5 and br_v6 in state UP, confirming the SVIs on the multilayer switch are healthy. There is no evidence of a switchport or tagging misconfiguration on the ms1_sw4 uplink.

Root cause: No current fault found; the helpdesk report appears stale or transient.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Advise Campus 2 users to re-test. If issues recur intermittently, capture timing and check sshswitch “ip addr” for br_v5/br_v6 status and switch4–sshswitch link ms1_sw4 for flaps at that time.
